  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Meadow coral fungus (Clavulinopsis corniculata) is a small, brightly colored fungal fruiting body belonging to the Clavulinaceae family, characterized by its distinctive branching structure resembling tiny antlers or coral. This edible species is found in grasslands and meadows throughout New England and other temperate regions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Yellow to orange coloration",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies display vibrant yellow to orange hues, sometimes with lighter tips",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Branching antler-like structure",
      "description": "Multiple slender branches arise from a common base, resembling miniature deer antlers or coral formations",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size and texture",
      "description": "Typically 1-4 inches tall with a brittle, slightly elastic texture that distinguishes it from similar species",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Meadow coral fungus thrives in open grasslands, meadows, and lawns throughout New England, preferring moist soil rich in organic matter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small stature, meadow coral fungus is edible and considered a delicacy in some regions, though it requires careful identification to avoid confusion with toxic look-alikes.",
